In article <1990Jun24.033819.10076@cs.uoregon.edu>>ghudson@cie.uoregon.edu (Greg Hudson) writes:
>     I love it!  Adds risk, too: if you wish for a Stormbringer, carry
>some missile weapons and be either invisible or able to jump: you're
>likely to find it in the hands of something on the order of a fire giant
>before you reach it.
>     I don't think that should be the case for God-granted wishes,
>though.  Perhaps spellbooks and such, but *all* the offering-related gifts
>are named weapons.  It's supposed to be a *good* thing to get something
>from your God, not a death trap (I've gotten Stormbringer as a 7th level
>character on dlvl 5.  Can you imagine trying to deal with a mordor orc
>wielding, say, Mjollnir?)

OK, how's this?

 o  Wands of wishing, escaped djinns, wishing wells, and other neutral
    wish sources will send the item far below (I prefer the levels between
    dlvl 25 and Hell, but others want it somewhere in the next 5 levels -
    I think it should be in a *very* dangerous place.   Wishes should not
    help you much while you are a low-level character that isn't strong enough
    to retrieve the item), to a random location anywhere on the level where it
    winds up.  Guard against the ploy of running up to dlvl 1 before wishing
    so the item doesn't go too deep.

 o  Items from a diety go at least 5 levels below where you currently are
    (if you haven't already descended to Hell - otherwise they can go
    anywhere above Hell).  But - these items are deposited inside a vault
    on the chosen level, with a vault teleport trap just like in a money
    vault.  If the levels are all mapped then a new vault will be created
    on the chosen level, along with a new hidden trap.  If you can't get
    out of the vault then you will have to drop the item and follow the
    guard (hint - the guard should be a priest of the indicated diety -
    ie - you don't want to mess with him).  I think somewhere 5 to 10
    levels below your furthest exploration would be good.

 o  A crystal ball will help you find any wished for item by telling you
    if it is far above or below, right above or below, or "near" (on the
    same level).

 o  For the randomly placed wishes, as I mentioned before, lots of items
    should be generated on the chosen level.  If you wish for blessed
    +3 gauntlets of power (which will show up as gloves of some kind),
    then there should be all sorts of gloves, maybe ten pairs or so, on
    the level where the gauntlets wind up.  Speed boots should result in
    a level of every kind of footwear that could be confusing.  They should
    be in all stages of blessing/cursing, so it will be unsafe to just try
    them all on blindly.  And the bogus stuff should purposely be generated
    with no plusses, but a good chance of minuses (so a wish doesn't outfit
    you with an infinite source of great armor).  So you will have to really
    work to retrieve the item.  But at least you will be working with some
    certainty that there is something valuable on the level.


This would especially slow down the "wish-for-wishes" ploy, since you have
to go find the new wand before you can use it.  Also, a new strategy would
be to wish for a crystal ball first, so that you would be equipped to find
future treasures, thus wasting 1 wish that is currently used for other
items.

Dangerous enemies would be unlikely to attack you with a weapon that is
hidden in a vault, although it could be deadly to enter a Stormbringer vault
containing a single orc.  Well, those are the breaks ;^).  New strategies
will be formulated to overcome this new danger, no doubt.

Djinns are notorious for double-edged wish granting, that is why I put
them in with the wand and the well.


